{"entries":{"chapter-43-study-falls|Chapter 43 Study: Falls":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"epidemiology|Epidemiology":["u3"],"copy|Fall = event resulting in a person coming to rest inadvertently on the ground/floor/lower level (p633).":["u4"],"copy|Community-dwelling 65+: fall incidence 32–40%; beyond 75: fall rates 40–50% (prospective studies; no time frame stated) (p633).":["u5"],"copy|Residential aged care (RACF) residents: fall incidence 30–56% (p633).":["u6"],"copy|Hospital falls: 2% (general hospitals) up to 27% (acute geriatric wards) (p633).":["u7"],"copy|37–56% of falls cause minor injury; 10–15% cause major injury (p633).":["u8"],"copy|Falls = 14% of ED admissions and 4% of all hospital admissions in 65+ (p633).":["u9"],"copy|Falls without injury still matter: fear of falling → reduced mobility/frailty and a key driver of institutionalization (p633).":["u10"],"risk-factors|Risk Factors":["u11"],"copy|Demographic: advancing age and female gender in the community; in hospital/institutional settings risk is similar or even higher in men (p634).":["u12"],"copy|Alcohol has NOT been shown to be a fall-risk factor in large cohort studies (despite causing osteoporosis/cerebellar atrophy/neuropathy) — likely underreporting/selection bias (p634).":["u13"],"copy|Medical: stroke, Parkinson disease, MS, dementia, depression, dizziness, incontinence, arthritis. Incontinence and cognitive impairment matter more in institutions; orthostatic hypotension and vestibular disorders aren't strong community risk factors but matter in specific populations (e.g., orthostatic hypotension in Parkinson disease) (p634).":["u14"],"copy|Medications: psychoactive drugs (benzodiazepine, antidepressant, antipsychotic) → 2–3x fall risk, ~2x hip-fracture risk (p634). NSAIDs: most recent systematic review found no significant increase on adjusted analysis (p636). Some antihypertensive classes (angiotensin system blocking medications) are protective; fall risk is significantly increased within the 24 hours after starting an antihypertensive or changing its dose (p636).":["u15"],"copy|Balance/gait: slower speed, shorter steps, wider step width, more double-support time predict falls; poor trunk/head control while walking impairs gaze stability (p636).":["u16"],"copy|Sensory/neuromuscular: reduced contrast sensitivity/depth perception and reduced knee-extension/ankle-dorsiflexor strength (especially in RACF) are risk factors (p636).":["u17"],"copy|Neuropsychological: poor dual-task performance (e.g., talking while walking), poor executive function/attention, fear of falling and depression → inactivity/isolation (p636).":["u18"],"copy|Environmental: it has been estimated that extrinsic factors are involved in 35–45% of injurious falls; 1/4 to 1/2 of falls occur away from home; a stairs fall carries ~2x the injury risk of other falls (p636–637).":["u19"],"copy|Table 43-1 rates each risk factor's association with falls on a */**/*** scale; use of ≥4 medications carries the same top rating (***) as psychoactive-drug use, and living alone/inactivity are rated ** (Table 43-1, p635).":["u20"],"screening-amp-assessment|Screening & Assessment":["u21"],"copy|Ask about falls in the last 12 months + assess balance/gait — previous falls is one of the strongest predictors of a future fall (p637).":["u22"],"copy|TUG test: rise from chair, walk 3 meters, turn, return, sit; a 25-study systematic review found limited predictive value in community-dwelling older adults and no cut-point for impaired performance could be recommended (p637).":["u23"],"copy|Alternatives with good predictive accuracy: Alternate Step Test, Sit-to-Stand Test, Short Physical Performance Battery (each domain scored 0–4, max total 12) (p637).":["u24"],"copy|Hospital (UK NICE): automatically assess all patients 65+, or 50–64 with a risk condition (e.g., recent stroke) (p637).":["u25"],"copy|RACF: CaHFRiS screen items — MMSE < 17, impulsivity, reduced standing balance, walking-frame use, a fall in the past year, antidepressant/hypnotic-anxiolytic use; absolute 6-month fall risk ranges 0% (no factors) → 100% (≥6 factors) (p637).":["u26"],"prevention-single-interventions|Prevention — Single Interventions":["u27"],"copy|Exercise: 23% fewer falls across 64 RCTs (community-dwelling); best results with high balance challenge + ≥3 hrs/week of prescribed exercise, effective even in 75+ (p638). Rapid-stepping training: 52% fewer falls (7-RCT meta-analysis) (p638). CAUTION — not a universal win: not effective in long-term stroke survivors or people just discharged from hospital; one home-based program increased falls by 43% in the recently-discharged (p639). In contrast: RACF resistance + balance training (25 wks) cut falls 55% (p639); a multifaceted RACF program (staff training/feedback + resident education + environmental adaptations + hip protectors + 2x/week group exercise) cut falls 45% (p639).":["u28"],"copy|Vision: first-eye cataract surgery → 34% fewer falls; second-eye surgery similar (~32%) but not statistically significant; one prospective cohort study associated cataract surgery with fewer falls provided post-op spectacle lens power updates were cautious (less than ±0.75 diopter change) (p639). Updating glasses to correct prescription: evidence less clear — one RCT found no significant reduction, and another (vision assessment + treatment) increased falls by ~50% (p639). Single-lens distance glasses plus counseling about ground-level hazards for habitual multifocal wearers: 40% fewer falls in the outdoor-active subgroup (p639). Home-safety modification for severe uncorrectable low vision: 41% fewer falls (p639).":["u29"],"copy|Medications: withdrawing centrally-acting drugs did NOT significantly reduce falls in the most recent systematic review (5 trials, 1309 participants; RaR 0.98, 95% CI 0.63–1.51) — better to prevent uptake than deprescribe later (p639). Avoid prescribing benzodiazepines/z-drugs/other psychotropics for insomnia unless there is a clear pattern of addiction or inability to complete a withdrawal program; consider nonpharmacological approaches (p639). Vitamin D: no benefit in community-dwelling older adults (even if deficient); may reduce falls in RACF residents (p639); Required reading: see AGS Beers 2023 required-article card for anticholinergics with prior falls or fractures (Table 3, p2066)":["u30"],"copy|Home safety: OT-delivered intensive interventions cut fall risk 21% overall, 39% in high-risk groups (p640); a cluster RCT of 842 households found home modifications cut fall-injury rate 26% overall and 39% for intervention-specific injuries (p640).":["u31"],"copy|Footwear/podiatry: multifaceted podiatry → 23% fewer falls; multifactorial interventions incorporating podiatry → 27% fewer falls (community) (p640); a RACF podiatry pilot was protective at 3 months but the effect was gone by 6 months (p641).":["u32"],"copy|Psychological: most CBT programs used alone have not prevented falls in people with fear of falling; adding CBT to multifactorial/exercise programs may help (p641).":["u33"],"copy|Cardiovascular: pacemaker implantation for cardioinhibitory carotid sinus hypersensitivity reduces drop attacks, syncope, and fall frequency (p641).":["u34"],"prevention-multifactorial|Prevention — Multifactorial":["u35"],"copy|Cochrane review of 43 community trials: multifactorial interventions → 23% fewer falls overall (p641).":["u36"],"copy|RACF: single-component interventions generally ineffective; multidisciplinary multifactorial programs with staff education work (p641).":["u37"],"copy|Two large primary-care trials found no significant reduction in their respective primary outcomes: fractures in PreFIT (9,803 participants) and serious fall injuries in STRIDE (5,451 participants). Direct delivery and uptake of the intervention may matter when implementing prevention through existing services (p641; PreFIT, STRIDE).":["u38"],"copy|CHAPTER 43s / HAZZARD":["u39"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 43 — Falls; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u40"]},"maxId":40}
